"- Transistor\n    - Type - Through-hole transistor with a black plastic body and three metallic leads.\n    - Color - Black plastic body with a circular marking containing a stylized \"T\" symbol; metallic leads are silver-colored.\n    - Size - Body approximately 10 mm in height and 5 mm in width; leads extend downward approximately 15 mm from the body.\n    - Material - Plastic body, metallic leads.\n    - Composition - Encapsulated semiconductor device with three metal pins for electrical connections.\n    - Quantity - One transistor.\n    - Relative Location - Positioned centrally in the image, mounted on the perforated board.\n    - Orientation - Body oriented vertically, leads pointing downward.\n    - Connection or Attachments - Leads soldered into three adjacent holes on the perforated board, forming electrical connections.\n\n- Solder Joint\n    - Type - Three solder joints connecting the transistor’s leads to the perforated board.\n    - Color - Metallic gray with some darkened areas from oxidation or residue.\n    - Size - Each joint approximately 2 mm in diameter at the point of connection.\n    - Material - Mixture of solder and metal from the transistor lead and board hole.\n    - Composition - Molten metal alloy (typically tin-lead or lead-free solder) fused to metal surfaces.\n    - Quantity - Three solder joints.\n    - Relative Location - Located at the base of each transistor lead, aligned with the corresponding holes on the board.\n    - Orientation - Each joint oriented vertically, connecting the downward-pointing leads to the board.\n    - Connection or Attachments - Each joint electrically connects one transistor lead to a copper trace via a hole in the board.\n\n- Copper Trace\n    - Type - Conductive copper paths on the perforated board.\n    - Color - Bright copper-colored with some darkened areas from oxidation or solder residue.\n    - Size - Width of each trace approximately 1 mm; length extends from each hole to the edges of the visible board area.\n    - Material - Copper metal.\n    - Composition - Pure copper or copper alloy, etched into the board to form conductive paths.\n    - Quantity - Multiple traces visible, forming a grid pattern across the board.\n    - Relative Location - Runs horizontally and vertically across the board, intersecting at the holes where components are mounted.\n    - Orientation - Parallel to each other, forming a grid-like pattern.\n    - Connection or Attachments - Each trace connects to a hole and serves as a conductive pathway for electrical current.\n\n- Hole\n    - Type - Circular through-hole in the perforated board for mounting components.\n    - Color - Dark brown or black due to oxidation and solder residue.\n    - Size - Diameter approximately 2.5 mm.\n    - Material - Copper metal, with surrounding board material.\n    - Composition - Copper-lined hole with solder residue and possibly some flux residue.\n    - Quantity - Multiple holes visible, arranged in a grid pattern.\n    - Relative Location - Located in a regular grid pattern on the board, with three holes directly under the transistor leads.\n    - Orientation - Circular, aligned with the copper traces.\n    - Connection or Attachments - Each hole connects to a copper trace and serves as a mounting point for the transistor’s leads.\n\n- Perforated Board\n    - Type - Printed circuit board (PCB) with a grid of holes and copper traces.\n    - Color - Copper-colored with darkened areas from oxidation and solder residue.\n    - Size - Visible portion approximately 50 mm wide and 50 mm tall.\n    - Material - Composite of copper traces and insulating substrate material (typically FR-4).\n    - Composition - Copper conductive traces on a non-conductive substrate, with holes for component mounting.\n    - Quantity - One board.\n    - Relative Location - Occupies the entire visible area of the image.\n    - Orientation - Horizontal and vertical grid pattern.\n    - Connection or Attachments - Copper traces